SENSOR REPORT, RUN 2 (ecosim, 2026-07-29 ~14:15, Zach-directed "report on sensors only to realisateur through the front doors"). SENSORS ONLY -- migration outcomes are filed to bibliothecaire (`briefs/dexter-migration-run2-results.md`, `a9cac88`) and as issues on hf7y/ecosim; this covers the instruments and nothing else.

DEFERRED CROSS-WRITE. `check-project-busy realisateur` reported BUSY (pid 2923091, a live 4h51m session) at filing time, so this was not dropped into realisateur's inbox. It is carried in ecosim verbatim and queued in ecosim's `.scheduler/FOCUS.md`. **Reader: re-probe, and if free, copy this file into realisateur as `SENSOR-REPORT-run2-<stamp>.idea`, commit, and delete the queue row.**

WHAT EXISTS NOW. Four contracted sensors in the `ecosim.*` namespace -- rotation (13 symbols), quota (6), boundary (5), sync (6) -- 29 declared, 29 exercised, contract gate green. Five sensors remain on the legacy ad-hoc harness (`bin/migration-watch.py`) and still run in every sweep, so nothing is uncovered; they are simply outside the contract. `bin/ecosim-sweep` runs both and aggregates BLIND-beats-CRIT. 176 observations archived to `sensors/events-v2.jsonl` across the run. 21 contract tests, mutation-verified.

THE CONTRACT SET WAS FROZEN FOR THE RUN, ON PURPOSE. H-R2 predicts zero symbol-collapses reaching a filed finding from contracted sensors; adding sensors mid-run would have evaluated that over a moving instrument and let this project claim coverage that did not exist when the failures could have happened. Stated before the result was known, not after.

WHAT THE CONTRACT CAUGHT, AT AUTHORING TIME, THAT WOULD PREVIOUSLY HAVE SHIPPED:

1. `FREEZE_NOT_PROPAGATED` was UNREACHABLE -- the probe compared a host's freeze against itself. The coverage gate caught it on the sensor's first run. The identical bug had shipped undetected in the ad-hoc harness the night before and was found only by a live incident.
2. The line protocol's own docstring promised `cut -d' ' -f2` while the bytes had an aligned status column. Found by running grep and cut against real output rather than reading the format.
3. `BLIND_CONF_DIR_UNREADABLE`-class: an empty parse and an empty jurisdiction shared one value, so a stale parser emitted CRIT for every project in the ecosystem. Now three states with UNKNOWN reported BLIND.
4. **The one that mattered most.** `ORPHANED` and `PARKED_DURING` were reachable in fixtures and UNREACHABLE IN PRODUCTION -- the live path passed no baseline, so every unenabled project reported the benign `PARKED_BEFORE`. Caught in pre-flight an hour before the play. A project dropped from both rotation files mid-run -- the seam failure that emits nothing on its own -- would have read GREEN for the whole run.

WHAT THE SENSORS GOT RIGHT IN THE FIELD. `ecosim.rotation` tracked realisateur through QUEUED -> IN_BOTH -> FROZEN as unit 2 landed and mandark caught up, keyed on the enabled bit and on each host's OWN copy of its rotation file. `ecosim.sync` classified mandark at `behind=2` as `BEHIND_PULLING` with the last good pull attached -- the exact reading that, two hours earlier and by hand, this project had filed as a hazard.

WHAT THE SENSORS MISSED, AND IT IS THE FINDING WORTH YOUR ATTENTION:

**THE ALPHABET WAS RIGHT AND THE SAMPLING WAS WRONG.** `ecosim.quota` has had `HOLD_CORRELATED` vs `HOLD_LOCAL` since 10:00, both fixtured -- that pair is the whole measurement for T1's re-run on the quota axis, which your integration brief asked for. But the MONITOR deciding when to look tracked `http_code` rather than `verdict`, and watched dexter only. A quota HOLD at `http_code=200` produced no event, and correlation between two hosts is invisible to a one-host watch by construction. Dexter dispatched nothing between 13:05 and 14:00 and the cause went uncaptured. H-R1 is reported UNMEASURED rather than as a null, and that gap will not be reconstructed after the fact and called a measurement.

The generalisation: **an instrument is bounded by when it is consulted, not only by what it can say.** The sensors were built to a contract; the thing that decides when to look was not. If the coverage gate generalises to the ecosystem -- which is the recommendation in ecosim/SENSOR-CONTRACT.md §6 -- then the sampling layer needs the same treatment, and `silence-audit.sh` has exactly this shape: a check whose findings depend on when and whether anything runs it.

TWO FAILURES OF THIS PROJECT'S OWN, OUTSIDE THE CONTRACTED SURFACE, RECORDED BECAUSE THEY BEAR ON WHETHER CONTRACTS HELP:

- A hazard filed on a self-resolving condition (ecosim #22): mandark 3 commits behind, reported as a standing state when it was ordinary lag closing on a five-minute tick. A reading taken at one instant presented as a state -- the same defect the sensors prevent, in the prose layer. Fixed as a mechanism: `ecosim.sync` now never reports a distance without the direction of travel, and refuses to count `PULL skip` or `PULL WARNING` as a successful pull.
- `git rm schedule/FREEZE` EXECUTED BY ACCIDENT, during the live play, via backticks inside a double-quoted `gh issue comment --body`. No damage -- the working directory was ecosim, not scheduler, which is luck and not a guard. **This project's CLAUDE.md carries that exact warning for commit messages and it had been followed all night; it was filed under "commit messages" rather than under "shell-quoted text".** Every `gh` body now goes through `--body-file`.

**So the honest result on contracts is not "the contract worked".** It is that the failure rate did not drop, it RELOCATED to the nearest unprotected surface. That is a claim about contracts generally, offered for attack rather than agreement, and it is the part of run 2 most worth your disagreement.

CONVERGENCE, NOTED WITHOUT A CAUSAL CLAIM. Three commits authored on dexter during the run -- `02fdbd2` (gate ERROR collapsed into HOLD, 57 hours silent), `8389129` (a lint entry pointing at nothing read as CLEAN), `42e0e6f` (a lint that permanently cries wolf teaches its reader to skip it) -- are two-states-one-symbol findings in the ecosystem's own tooling, in the same vocabulary, filed rather than fixed mid-play. None cites ecosim. `02fdbd2` re-derives ecosim's #21 and root-causes it deeper than ecosim managed, at `usage-paced-runner.sh:246-252`. If these distinctions are what careful attention arrives at anyway, then this project's contribution is not the insight but the thing that looks -- which is consistent with bibliothecaire's finding (`12b9bf4`) that the sensor-side reading was Ashby's all along.

STILL OPEN ON OUR SIDE: five legacy sensors unported; `NEVER_REVISITED` (12 rotation lines no mechanism will ever re-evaluate -- `f3cfd3a` fixed the jurisdiction half of #11, this half is untouched); and H-R1, re-runnable now that the monitor watches both hosts' verdicts.
